Hello everyone,

I am analyzing data from a migrating bird study conducted in 7 habitats. =
I=20
surveyed from 10-20 transects within each habitat during fall and spring=20=

over 2 years. My goals are to get overall densities of birds in each=20
habitat/season as well as obtain densities of certain guilds in these=20
habitats. I detected over 200 species of birds, and often there are not=20=

enough detections of any one species in any one habitat to get a reliable=
=20
detection function. Thus grouping birds with similar detection=20
probabilities was necessary for each habitat.
=20
I plan to use a global detection function and post=96stratify by species =
to=20
obtain density estimates. Is the CDS or MCDS engine more appropriate for=20=

this approach? The survey was designed to reduce effects of some potentia=
l=20
covariates such as wind speed and other adverse weather.  I tried the MCD=
S=20
engine with species as a covariate, but I=92m not sure this makes sense w=
ith=20
many species having very few observations. I had trouble fitting the mode=
l=20
with convergence failure and parameters hitting bounds. I don=92t underst=
and=20
the theory enough to set starting parameter values to fix these issues.=20=

The bootstrapping summary also reported all densities as zero.

I=92ve tried the CDS engine with and without bootstrapping. In many cases=
=20
candidate models had AIC values that varied by less than 2, so I tried=20=

bootstrapping with various combinations of models. It seems to select a=20=

certain model, so how is it model averaging? I understand that=20
bootstrapping gives more reliable estimates of variance  because it=20
considers uncertainty in model selection, but should I also be reporting=20=

the density estimates in the bootstrap summary instead of those I obtaine=
d=20
without bootstrapping?=20

Any help would be greatly appreciated!

